INT. STAGECOACH WAY STATION - SUPPER - NIGHT

A low adobe room. One long table, a smoking lamp, a cracked piano that no one has played in years. Wind rattles the shutters.

CALLOWAY DREW (40s, dusty duster, tin star pinned inside the collar) sits at the head of the table, eating beans without hurry. Beside him, ABIGAIL STROUD (30s, widow's black, gloves still on) pushes her plate away. Across from them, REUBEN TALLCHIEF (50s, stagecoach driver, scarred hands) cuts bread. HOMER PRICE (20s, station boy, apron too long) fills cups. MAGDALENA SOTO (40s, cook, sleeves rolled) stands at the stove with her back to the room.

CALLOWAY
Good beans.

MAGDALENA
Same beans as last week.

CALLOWAY
Then I was wrong last week.

REUBEN
The coach is late.

CALLOWAY
It's always late.

REUBEN
Not this late.

ABIGAIL
Perhaps the road is bad.

REUBEN
Road's the same as always, ma'am.

HOMER
Mr. Tallchief says there's something moving out past the dry wash. He saw it from the roof.

REUBEN
I said no such thing, boy.

HOMER
You said "somebody."

MAGDALENA
Homer. Cups.

Homer pours. His hand shakes. A little coffee spills on the table.

CALLOWAY
Easy.

HOMER
Yes, sir.

ABIGAIL
Marshal, what is a man like you doing at a way station?

CALLOWAY
Eating beans.

ABIGAIL
That is not an answer.

CALLOWAY
It's the true one.

MAGDALENA
He's waiting on somebody.

CALLOWAY
Magdalena.

MAGDALENA
What? She asked.

REUBEN
I'll tell you what he's waiting on. He's waiting on whoever shot Harlan Boyce at the Platte crossing.

ABIGAIL
^I did.

REUBEN
^She did.

They say it at the same instant, one with certainty, one with dread. The wind stops. The lamp flame stands straight.

Calloway sets his spoon down.

CALLOWAY
Ma'am?

Abigail pulls off one glove. Lays it on the table. Then the other.

ABIGAIL
Harlan Boyce killed my husband. I waited eleven years. I was a very patient woman.

Nobody moves. Homer drops the pot. It rings on the floor like a church bell.

CALLOWAY
Magdalena, lock the door.

MAGDALENA
It's already locked.

CALLOWAY
Then lock it again.
